From: David Hamner <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wininet: a listen timeout is accepted on any handle

Office's HTTP client (OneAuth) sets four timeouts on every connection
handle it opens: connect, send, receive and listen. wininet refused the
listen timeout (option 11, INTERNET_OPTION_LISTEN_TIMEOUT) with
ERROR_INTERNET_INVALID_OPTION, and OneAuth takes any refused option for
a failed request: its calls to Office's services (the connected
experiences and licensing hosts) never went out, and the sign-in dialog
ended with "Something went wrong", code 12009. The option only matters
to a listening socket, and Windows accepts it on any handle, so it is
accepted and ignored.

Gate: test/wininet-listentimeout-gate.sh (the option on a connection
handle, next to the three OneAuth sets with it). Mutant:
SG_MUTANT_WININET_NO_LISTEN_TIMEOUT.
---
--- a/dlls/wininet/internet.c
+++ b/dlls/wininet/internet.c
@@ -3468,6 +3468,13 @@
 
     switch (dwOption)
     {
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_WININET_NO_LISTEN_TIMEOUT
+    case INTERNET_OPTION_LISTEN_TIMEOUT:
+        /* Stained Glass OS (wine-sg 1477): only used for listening sockets, but Windows accepts it on any
+         * handle, and Office's HTTP client (OneAuth) treats a refusal as a failed request */
+        TRACE("INTERNET_OPTION_LISTEN_TIMEOUT: ignored\n");
+        break;
+#endif
     case INTERNET_OPTION_HTTP_VERSION:
       {
         HTTP_VERSION_INFO* pVersion=(HTTP_VERSION_INFO*)lpBuffer;
